{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CezcuzzBQcDpZ6JhZF6EJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When the rider squeezes the brake lever, they pull a steel cable that runs from the lever to the brake mechanism. This cable is under tension and transmits the pulling force directly to the brake caliper, which is mounted near the wheel.

The cable's tension pulls the caliper arms inward, forcing the brake pads toward the rim. As the pads press against the rim's sides, friction between the rubber pads and the metal rim creates resistance that slows the wheel's rotation. The harder the rider squeezes the lever, the greater the cable tension and the stronger the braking force applied to the rim."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":144,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}

